docs/phase-1-plan.md:56:## 2. Jobs (Solid Queue, `config/recurring.yml`, all times America/New_York)
docs/phase-1-plan.md:58:**Token model (revised after the step 4 counselors review).** Pause state is *derived* from the current token, never written by a failure. `schwab_tokens` gains `revoked_at`; `SchwabToken.current` is the newest by `issued_at, id`; `usable?` = present, not refresh-expired (`issued_at + 168.hours`, elapsed time not calendar days), not revoked. `SchwabToken.issue!(response)` inserts the new row and clears `system_settings.sync_paused_reason` in one transaction, enqueuing `Sync::CatchUpJob` after commit. `SchwabToken#refresh!` takes the row lock, reloads, and returns early if the row is no longer current or was refreshed in the last minute (so two workers cannot double-refresh and a stale row cannot act); it builds the client inside the lock; `access_expires_at` = now + `expires_in` − 60 s; `refresh_token` = response value or the existing one; `refresh_expires_at` is never touched. On `ReauthRequired` it sets `revoked_at` on the current row only, records `last_refresh_error` outside the lock so a rollback cannot lose it, audits `token_refresh_failed`, and sets `sync_paused_reason`. On `ServerError`/`TransportError` it records the error and does nothing else. `ensure_fresh!(margin:)` refreshes proactively when the access token is near expiry, so a 401 is the safety net rather than the norm. `Schwab.client` raises `Schwab::NoUsableToken` rather than letting nil reach a request.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:60:**Token gating.** Only `Sync::TickJob` and the agent jobs are scheduled; everything else is enqueued by them. When `SchwabToken.current` is not usable, the tick job writes one `sync_runs` `skipped_token` row per hour, audits `sync_paused_token_expired` once per episode, and enqueues nothing. No self-re-enqueue, so the queue cannot grow. The re-auth callback (`issue!`) clears the pause, audits `sync_resumed`, and enqueues `Sync::CatchUpJob`, which runs all feeds with windows widened to cover the gap (capped at the 60-day limit). Agent jobs run degraded rather than wait: `degraded: true`, the prompt states the token is unusable and every number carries its as-of time, the email subject is prefixed, and the token status line sits at the top of every brief so a dead nag path is still visible daily. Snapshots are not written for days without a fresh balance.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:62:**Re-auth flow.** `SchwabController#authorize` (login required) stores a random `state` with a timestamp in the session and redirects to the authorize URL. `#callback` (login required; an unauthenticated hit is rejected without storing a return-to URL, so the code never lands in the session) reads and deletes the pending state; no pending state renders "nothing pending, start again" with no audit; a mismatch audits `re_auth_failed` as CSRF; `params[:error]` (user cancelled) renders the failure without calling the token endpoint. On success: `exchange_code`, then `/accounts/accountNumbers` must overlap the known `accounts` hashes (first auth accepts anything) or the token is not made current and `re_auth_failed` is audited; then `issue!`, audit `re_auth_succeeded`, and **redirect** to a clean success page so a browser refresh cannot resubmit the single-use code. A failed exchange never writes to `schwab_tokens`. The retry link goes to `authorize`. `code`, `state`, and `session` join `filter_parameters`. The callback URL comes only from `SCHWAB_CALLBACK_URL`, never from the request. `fake_authorize` 404s unless the adapter is fake and the environment is not production, and `?fail=1` redirects with a bad code for Playwright.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:64:**Health and nags.** `Schwab::TokenHealthJob` runs 08:00 and 20:00 ET. Nag when: no usable token (absent, revoked, or expired), sent at every run; or the 24-hour warning, sent once per token row (deduped on token id in the audit data). Monday/Tuesday escalation fires when `SchwabToken.current.issued_at` is before the most recent Sunday 18:00 ET, which is the PRD's literal "the Sunday re-auth hasn't happened"; no buffer, no comparison against the coming Sunday. Cron strings carry `America/New_York` explicitly. The refresh task gets a higher queue priority than sync.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:68:| `Schwab::RefreshAccessTokenJob` | every 25 min, high priority | `SchwabToken.current&.refresh!`; no-op when there is no usable token |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:69:| `Schwab::TokenHealthJob` | daily 08:00 + 20:00 | See "Health and nags" above. Audits `token_nag_sent` with the token id and reason. |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:70:| `Sync::TickJob` | every 5 min | Sole scheduled sync entry point. Refreshes `market_days` for today (New York) plus 7 days when missing or `source: fallback` (one call per date; a market-hours failure never aborts the tick). Due checks compare against the last *started* run per account and feed with 1-minute slack, and a `running` run is never due; jobs also declare Solid Queue `limits_concurrency` keyed on account and feed so backlog cannot double-enqueue. Cadences apply to trading-enabled accounts: positions (15 min inside the session window pre-market through post-market, 60 min otherwise), orders and activities (5 min inside, 60 outside); read-only accounts sync hourly. `Snapshots::DailyJob` once per market day after `regular_closes_at` + 20 min, only when that day's calendar row came from Schwab. |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:72:| `Sync::PositionsJob(account)` | via tick | `/accounts/{hash}?fields=positions`; resolve instruments (§2 "Instrument identity"); upsert positions; append a `balances` row; delete positions absent from the response only when the run is complete (every position resolved) and the payload is sane (no positions with positive long market value is a `failed` run, not an empty account); then `Journal::BootstrapJob` for trading-enabled accounts |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:73:| `Sync::OrdersJob(account)` | via tick | Window: from = max(last `window_to` for this account − 1 day, now − 59 days), to = end of the current UTC day (bounds may be date-granular; verify item). First sync backfills 59 days. Flatten nested orders and upsert in two passes (rows, then lineage by broker id); container strategies are stored with no instrument; exactly 3000 rows means truncation and finishes `partial`. Any `positions.working_stop_order` not present in the response is re-fetched by id (covers old GTC stops, out-of-band cancels, and replacements outside the window). A daily `status: WORKING` pass. Then working-stop detection (§5) for every position, only on a complete run; a failed or partial run leaves stop assignments untouched |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:74:| `Sync::ActivitiesJob(account)` | via tick | One call per transaction type the cash account can produce (TRADE, RECEIVE_AND_DELIVER, DIVIDEND_OR_INTEREST, ELECTRONIC_FUND, ACH_RECEIPT, ACH_DISBURSEMENT, WIRE_IN, WIRE_OUT, JOURNAL, CASH_RECEIPT, CASH_DISBURSEMENT, MONEY_MARKET), cursor per account and feed: from = max(last `window_to` − 3 days, now − 59 days), to = end of the current UTC day. A `RequestError` on one type skips that type and finishes `partial`; 429, 5xx, and transport failures leave the cursor alone. Upsert by `broker_activity_id`, link `order_id` via the activity's orderId, classify `kind` (§2 "Activity kinds"); then `Journal::LinkFillsJob` |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:75:| `Sync::CatchUpJob` | after re-auth | Bypasses due checks: accounts, then per account positions, orders (with the WORKING pass), activities. Windows already derive from the last `window_to`, so a short pause needs nothing special; when the computed start is later than the last success, the run records `gap_from` and audits `sync_gap_unrecoverable` so nobody believes the catch-up was complete |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:84:| `Agent::SundayReviewJob` | Sunday 18:00 | Phase 1 stub, no API call: re-auth link, token age, sync health, job failures, setup stats table |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:86:**Sync runs.** `SyncRun.record(feed, account:, window:)` wraps every feed: creates the row, captures `window_to` before the request, rescues `NoUsableToken`, `AuthError`, and `ReauthRequired` into `skipped_token`, rescues other `Schwab::Error` into `failed`, counts quarantined records into `partial` with `unmapped_values` (raw type, description, subAccount, sign, item shape), and stamps `records_touched`. `partial` advances the cursor; `failed` does not. A cash-affecting `other` activity makes the run `partial`, never a quiet success.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:88:**Instrument identity** (`Instrument.resolve(ref)`): CUSIP, then Schwab instrument id, then symbol plus asset type **matching only rows with no CUSIP**, then an `instrument_by_symbol` lookup, then create a provisional row. A weaker-key match backfills a missing CUSIP or instrument id from the ref but never overwrites a differing one. A CUSIP match with a different symbol calls `rename!`. The `instrument_identity_fallback` audit fires once, when a provisional row is created, not on every resolve. `RecordNotUnique` is handled with a re-lookup because positions and activities race.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:90:**Activity kinds.** TRADE with an EQUITY security → `fill`; TRADE with a CASH_EQUIVALENT security or MONEY_MARKET → `sweep`; DIVIDEND_OR_INTEREST → `dividend` when a security is present, else `interest`; ELECTRONIC_FUND, ACH_*, WIRE_*, CASH_* and JOURNAL with only currency items → `deposit` or `withdrawal` by sign; JOURNAL or RECEIVE_AND_DELIVER with one security → `transfer` (ACAT deliveries are not corporate actions), with two securities → `corporate_action`; all items fees → `fee`; anything else → `other` and counted. Activities whose `status` is not VALID are stored and never journaled.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:92:**Market calendar.** `MarketDay.for(date)` reads the row or fetches it (source schwab). When the token is unusable or the call fails it writes a weekday-open 9:30 to 16:00 row with `source: fallback`, which drives cadence only: snapshots and market-day briefs require a Schwab-sourced row, and fallback rows are re-fetched on every tick until they flip. "Today" is computed in America/New_York.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:94:**Schwab client:** built to `docs/schwab-api-contract.md`. Net::HTTP; plaintext account numbers scrubbed inside the client before any body is returned; logs carry path templates (`/accounts/{hash}/orders`), never hashes, bodies, or headers; one time helper for the three input and three output formats; `BigDecimal` for every money and quantity value; a shared Solid Cache rate budget of 100 requests/minute across Puma and the worker; retries on 429, 5xx, and transport errors for GETs and the refresh grant only (1, 4, 16 s with jitter, `Retry-After` capped at 60 s), never on 4xx; one refresh-and-retry on a 401. Error classes: `AuthError`, `ReauthRequired`, `RateLimited`, `ServerError`, `RequestError`, `TransportError`, `UnknownOutcome` (non-idempotent calls only, chosen by HTTP method), `MappingError`. Interface adds `order(hash, order_id)` so working stops older than the lookback are re-fetched by id, and `instrument(symbol_or_cusip)` for identity fallback. Mappers return `Data` structs with a scrubbed `raw` member; jobs never see a Schwab field name. `Schwab::FakeClient` sits below the mappers and returns raw-shaped hashes with a scriptable per-call queue. Sync runs can finish `partial` with an `unmapped_values` count when individual records are quarantined.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:104:| `get_portfolio` | Positions with last price, unrealized P&L, working stop, distance to stop, per-position open risk, `unprotected` flag, synced_at per row |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:105:| `get_account_health` | Equity, settled/unsettled cash, drawdown from HWM (index-based), unprotected count, token status and age, last sync per feed, shadow mode, halt state, sync pause reason |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:106:| `get_quotes(symbols)` | Schwab `/marketdata/v1/quotes`, cached 2 min in Solid Cache, each quote stamped with fetched_at; when the token is expired returns the cached value with `stale: true` or an explicit unavailable error |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:116:**Scheduled runtime:** `Agent::Run` wraps one Messages API conversation: system prompt = memory repo markdown files + setup stats + token health + as-of timestamps; tools = scheduled-allowed registry; hard cap `MAX_TOOL_TURNS = 12`; result stored in `agent_runs` and emailed via `BriefMailer`. Prompts live in `app/models/agent/prompts/*.md.erb`. The morning-brief prompt states that earnings and economic-calendar data are unavailable in Phase 1 and must not be inferred. Morning brief and EOD are real; Sunday review stub does not call the API.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:148:- Partially protected: remaining stop quantity below the position quantity. Shown as such, counted as unprotected in the portfolio total.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:149:- Oversized: remaining stop quantity above the position quantity. Schwab will reject the whole order at activation in a cash account, so this is flagged unprotected with its own reason (PRD §5.4.2 hard-alert case).
docs/phase-1-plan.md:151:- No stop, or a stop no longer working: unprotected.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:152:- Portfolio open risk sums protected positions only, and the dashboard and `get_account_health` state how many positions are excluded and why.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:163:- `open_risk_amount`, `open_risk_pct`, `unprotected_count` from §5 at snapshot time.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:172:| Dashboard | `/` | Banner (healthy / expiring / expired / halted), equity + day change, open risk with unprotected count, settled/unsettled cash, positions list with distance-to-stop as the primary number and inline `unprotected` where applicable, "N trades need review" callout, pending proposals count (0), staleness stamps on every number, empty state before first sync |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:175:| System health | `/system` | Token age vs 7d with next Sunday marked, last sync per feed, sync pause reason, job failures (7d), API spend (month, from `agent_runs`), shadow mode indicator, manual halt toggle with reason (audited) |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:177:| Login | `/session/new` → email → `/session/magic/:token` | Passwordless; unknown emails get the same "check your inbox" response |
docs/phase-1-plan.md:187:- **RSpec**, in the kickoff's priority order: token lifecycle with `travel_to` (25-minute refresh, 7-day expiry unaffected by refreshes, pause and catch-up, Mon/Tue escalation rule); sync jobs with fixture payloads (positions incl. removal, orders incl. cancel/replace and working stops, transactions incl. cash dividend, split, return of capital, CUSIP succession, fee items); journal linking (scale-in/out, orphan fill, sell-first legacy holding, bootstrap, ticker change on same CUSIP, R math); snapshot math (flows, late-landing deposit rebuild); MCP tool contracts (auth, each tool's schema and output, permission metadata excludes non-scheduled tools); scheduled runtime with a stubbed Messages API (turn cap, degraded mode).
docs/phase-1-plan.md:188:- **VCR cassettes** in `spec/cassettes`, recorded against Schwab only once the app is Ready For Use, with a scrubber that replaces bearer tokens, refresh tokens, and account hash values before write. Until then, cassettes are hand-built from community-client response shapes and marked `# SCHWAB_SHAPE_UNVERIFIED`.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:200:- App time zone `Eastern Time (US & Canada)`; Solid Queue recurring schedules use the same.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:209:4. OAuth controller + token model + refresh job + token health job + re-auth pages + pause/catch-up + audit events.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:232:- Orders endpoint supports `fromEnteredTime`/`toEnteredTime` and a `status` filter; maximum lookback window.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:238:- Refresh token expiry is 7 days from issuance and is not extended by access-token refreshes.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:11:4. Which token-endpoint `error` value and HTTP status Schwab sends for a 7-day-expired refresh token.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:12:5. Whether the refresh grant returns a new `refresh_token` string or the same one.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:13:6. That refreshing never extends the 7-day refresh-token window.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:17:8. Exact wrapper nesting of `/accounts/{hash}` (`securitiesAccount`, `aggregatedBalance`) and that `hashValue` is absent from `/accounts`.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:25:14. Orders lookback: 60 days (schwab-py) or one year (Schwabdev). Probe 61+ days.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:32:19. Transactions lookback (60 days versus one year and 3000 rows) and whether `startDate` beyond it returns 400.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:46:29. Actual per-minute read limit, and whether it is per app, per token, or per account.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:50:31. Refresh-token rotation under a dropped connection: refresh, kill the connection mid-response, refresh again. If the second call returns `invalid_grant`, the refresh grant is not safely retriable and the client must stop retrying it.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:51:32. Whether issuing a new token via the authorization-code grant invalidates the previous refresh token immediately.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:56:35. Whether `/orders/{orderId}` returns orders older than the list lookback.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:58:37. Orders list with `status=WORKING` and a one-year lookback (Schwabdev claims it works); this is how GTC stops older than 60 days become visible on first sync.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:63:35. Whether `/orders/{orderId}` returns orders older than the list lookback.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:65:37. Orders list with `status=WORKING` and a one-year lookback (Schwabdev claims it works); this is how GTC stops older than 60 days become visible on first sync.
